trace.content:
[THOUGHT] The patient cannot reliably report distribution — so direct clinical observation remains essential. Prior exam notes confirmed well-demarcated, non-scaly, non-inflammatory patches on face, hands, and arms (sun-exposed areas), but key diagnostic features like *perifollicular repigmentation* (a sign of early repigmentation in vitiligo) and *leukotrichia* (white hairs within patches — highly specific for vitiligo) were not assessed or reported. These are easily observable during physical exam and carry high diagnostic weight: leukotrichia strongly favors vitiligo over post-inflammatory hypopigmentation or tinea; perifollicular repigmentation suggests disease stability or response. Since physical exam is underutilized and these items are unaddressed, I must request them now.

[TEST] Perform targeted physical examination of the lightened patches to assess for:  
- Presence of white hairs (leukotrichia) within or at the margins of the patches;  
- Perifollicular repigmentation (small dots of pigment around hair follicles inside the patches);  
- And whether the depigmented areas appear truly chalk-white (complete loss of pigment) versus off-white (residual pigment).

